  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What to check first</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Who is in the thread</strong>Customer and staff messages may contain personal details that need review.</li>
        <li><strong>Terms of use</strong>Confirm the tool&rsquo;s terms allow your company to share and license the content.</li>
        <li><strong>Time span</strong>Records from several years are usually more useful than one quarter.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a thread teaches a model</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A transcript is only worth licensing when it shows the reasoning, not just the ending. The value sits in how a vague request is narrowed, who settles the question, and what happens when the first answer turns out to be wrong.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>How a request gets specific</strong>The follow-up questions an agent asks before anyone commits to an answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Where authority sits</strong>Which reply actually closed the question, and who was allowed to give it.</li>
        <li><strong>How a decision gets reversed</strong>A fix that was approved and then changed, with the reason recorded in the thread.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What keeps conversations out of scope</h2>
      <p class="reveal">These are the reasons a set of threads is normally dropped or cut back. Each is checked before any buyer sees the records.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>A recording nobody was told about</strong>Where law requires both parties to know a call is recorded, a transcript without that notice is not usable.</li>
        <li><strong>Client confidentiality clauses</strong>An engagement letter or master agreement can forbid sharing correspondence with a third party.</li>
        <li><strong>Material pasted in from elsewhere</strong>Third-party documents quoted into a thread carry their own terms.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

